<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of St. Lawrence</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> is a large inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> at the mouth of the St. Lawrence River. French explorers, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a>, entered it in the sixteenth century. It served as the gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and the North American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> overseas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-st-lawrence.jpg" alt="Gulf of St. Lawrence" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nichole Ouellette/ouellette001.com Camera location49° 19′ 32.1″ N, 67° 22′ 02.3″ W View this and other nearby images ... · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baie_Trinite_023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
